%I A402563 #4 Nov 26 2026 20:09:47
%S A402563 2,13,31,1223,37898039
%N A402563 Primes p such that Product_{primes q < p} (q-1) == -1 (mod p).
%C A402563 Let p = prime(n). Then p is a term if and only if p divides A005867(n-1) + 1, or equivalently A005867(n) - 1.
%C A402563 This is the analog of A066735 with the product of the primes q < p replaced by the product of q-1. The analog of A341804 is A402562.
%C A402563 2 is a term because the empty product is 1, and 1 == -1 (mod 2).
%C A402563 a(5) = 37898039 = prime(2313146).
%C A402563 The terms p with pi(p) odd (pi = A000720), namely 2 and 31 below 10^11, also satisfy Product_{primes q < p} (1-q) == -1 (mod p); see A402562.
%C A402563 Heuristically, the number of terms below x should grow like log(log(x)); see A402562.
%C A402563 a(6) > 10^11 (same search as in A402562).
%e A402563 13 is a term: (2-1)*(3-1)*(5-1)*(7-1)*(11-1) = 480 = 13*37 - 1, so the product == -1 (mod 13).
%e A402563 11 is not a term: (2-1)*(3-1)*(5-1)*(7-1) = 48 = 4*11 + 4, so the product == 4 (mod 11).
%e A402563 31 is a term: the product of q-1 over the 10 primes q < 31 is A005867(10) = 1021870080 = 31*32963551 - 1.
%o A402563 (PARI) isok(p) = if(!isprime(p), return(0)); my(w=Mod(1, p)); forprime(q=2, p-1, w *= q-1); w == -1;
%o A402563 (PARI) lista(nn) = my(t=1); forprime(p=2, nn, if(t%p == p-1, print1(p, ", ")); t *= p-1);
%o A402563 (Python)
%o A402563 from sympy import primerange
%o A402563 def aupto(limit):
%o A402563     t, alst = 1, []
%o A402563     for p in primerange(2, limit+1):
%o A402563         if t % p == p - 1: alst.append(p)
%o A402563         t *= p - 1
%o A402563     return alst
%o A402563 print(aupto(10**5))
%Y A402563 Cf. A000720, A002110, A005867, A062347, A066735, A341804, A402562.
%K A402563 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A402563 1,1
